"""The packs of your own games: music per game, by ear, then the packs, on a terminal screen or
without one (BUILDING.md, "Your own games").

Point it at a folder of ZX Spectrum games (.sna, .z80, .tap, .tzx). Per game, mark one or more
ways to make its music, listen to the results, choose one per score, and build: the studio splits
the games into as many packs as they need, each a full pair of update packages (fmzx.fwsc and
fmzx-preview.fwsc) with each game's chosen music. It needs Python 3.11 or later and nothing
else of its own: run it as tools/pack-studio (through uv) or python3 tools/pack-studio.

  tools/pack-studio <games folder | games file> [-o W] [--sid-dir PATH] [--ay-dir PATH] [--offline]
                    [--cache DIR] [--model NAME]
      Opens the studio (curses). The games are only read; everything goes under the workspace W,
      build/studio/<folder name> unless -o says otherwise (build/ is git-ignored).
      At start, in the background: fmzx-pack and music-preview (cd host && cargo build
      --release; when music-preview does not build, the studio plays the renders
      tools/arrange-music wrote instead), then the learning run (below). Both device builds
      (tools/fm1-docker cmake --preset fm1 and fm1-preview, then cmake --build with each, in
      Docker: a no-op when they are up to date, but a few seconds) run before each build (b,
      --build), and at start only when their app images are not there yet (the learning needs
      them for the room they leave); FMZX_SKIP_DEVICE_BUILD=1 skips them, and build/fm1 and
      build/fm1-preview must then exist. The learning run:
          fmzx-pack personal <folder> -o W/all --app <fm1 app> --app <fm1-preview app> --plan
      which converts and learns every game and writes W/all/personal-games.toml (the folder's
      games file: a re-run keeps your edits to it), the scores in W/all/arrange/ and
      W/all/plan.json (each game: how it went in, its packed size with its chosen music, its
      music line and its scores). It reports each game on stderr as it goes (fmzx-pack's
      "loaded 3/45: <file> (<how>)", "converted 3/45: <name> (<how>)", "learning 45 games: 27
      from the cache, 18 to learn, 8 at a time", "learning: <name>", "learned 13/45: <name>
      (21.4 s)" or "(cached)", "checking the chosen takes of 17 games", "checked 3/17: <name>
      (1.4 s)": each game's steps); until the plan is there the games screen lists the games
      (W/all/personal-games.toml's, or the folder's game files before it is written), each with
      a glyph for its state, explained on the line above the list: · waiting, ▸ converting or
      learning (yellow), ✓ learned (green; dim green from the cache), ✗ left out (with why;
      . > + x without UTF-8), its own bar while it has work in hand (below), else its converted
      file's size (~, before packing); the header says "learning…". Before a
      workspace's first learning (no W/all/plan.json), a job copies
      the cache files W/all/sound-cache lacks from the sound-cache/ folders (not links) of other
      personal packs under build/ (at most four folders down, beside a personal-games.toml or a
      converted/manifest.toml, none inside W): fmzx-pack names a cache file
      <crc>-<learner version>.bin, the CRC over the converted game, its key script and its
      overrides, so a copy is the same game's music. A file there is never overwritten; the log
      says "reused 27 learned games from build/zx-tunes" per folder.
      A games file (.toml, the syntax of W/all/personal-games.toml) in place of the folder is
      used as written: the learning reads it (fmzx-pack personal <file> -o W/all ..., a relative
      file from the games file's folder), nothing rewrites it, and the packs take their entries
      from it. Its workspace is the one it lies in when it is a workspace's
      all/personal-games.toml (after you deleted or commented out some entries there), else
      build/studio/<file name without .toml>.
      Long steps run one at a time in the background. Nothing a tool prints shows on the screen
      (nor without it): every line goes to W/studio.log (the log, below). What shows is progress,
      as bars of squares (■ done, □ to go; # and . without UTF-8):
      g, m and M queue their work game by game (a game's AI, SID and AY jobs, then its judge,
      then the next game's; a collection's download first), so games finish one at a time; the
      queue runs in the order submitted, a job waiting for those it is after (a game's judge for
      its SID and AY jobs) and a job held for a download taking its place again once released.
      Per game, on the games list: a game with work in hand in the current activity (the jobs
      since the queue was last empty) has a bar in place of its columns, as wide as the row
      leaves (4 to 20 cells), then what is in hand, dim: loading, converting, learning, checking
      (its chosen takes), SID takes, AY takes, AI takes, judging, pack <n>. Its share: each of its
      steps (its learning, each takes job, the judge, its pack's fmzx-pack run and packages) a
      share, done (1) or by its measure while it runs: the learning its load (a tape loaded),
      conversion, learning and check (when it has chosen takes) done of those known, each counted
      when its line comes, not before; a SID or AY job its candidates checked of those found; an
      AI job (take - 1 + the take's share done) / takes; the judge its takes rendered and scores
      judged of those its progress lines plan (tools/arrange-music's docstring, "The progress
      lines"); a pack's fmzx-pack run that game's steps in it; any other step 0 until done. A game
      with work to come but none in hand (not begun, or between its steps) is queued: its
      columns, "queued" dim in place of its tunes, no bar. A game whose work is done shows its
      columns again; one whose step failed a red mark in place of its choices, "✗ SID takes failed
      (l: the log)", until a later job for it is done.
      The whole, on the bottom line of every screen: what the activity does (finding and judging,
      finding C64 versions, making AI takes, learning games, building pack 2 of 3; never a game's
      name), its bar (each game in it a share, as above, and each step without a game: a download
      its MB, the tools' and device builds, the reuse, renders and the judge's final pass done or
      not), "12 of 31 games" (the games done of those in it), the time it has run, and the time
      left where it can be estimated (the learning's estimate; for work done game by game, once 3
      games are done, their average time a game), e.g.
          finding and judging ■■■■■■■■■■■□□□□□□□□□□□□□□□ 12 of 31 games  4:10  ~6 min
          downloading SID collection ⠹ 0:03 ■■■■■□□□□□□□ 0 of 4 games
      A running step without a game whose progress is not known (a download without its size,
      the device and tools' builds, the judge's final pass) shows its own label, a spinner and
      the time it has run (in place of the activity's); a lone such step (a render on its own)
      shows only its label, the spinner and that time ("rendering a preview ⠹ 0:03"). On a
      narrow screen the time left goes first, then the figures; the bar keeps at least 4 cells.
      The bar is yellow while the
      activity runs; when it is over it stays 3 s, full and green when done, red where it
      stopped when a step failed ("failed (J: the jobs, l: the log)").
      Messages (what g queued, a finished takes job's result, a refusal) take the key line above
      it for 3 s, in a coloured band; with nothing running the bottom line says "idle" and the
      last message. J lists every job (the jobs view, below).
      The log, W/studio.log: a dated line per session ("-- 2026-01-02 14:03:10 tools/pack-studio
      <its arguments> (workspace W)"), then a line per event, "HH:MM:SS <tag> <text>", the tag
      in a column of its own: a job's number, kind and game ("#4 sid alpha", "#2 learn"), or
      "studio". A job's lines: "queued: <title>" (held: why), "start: <title>: <its command line,
      extra environment first>", each line it prints as "| <line>", "end: done, exit 0, 41 s" (or
      failed, with the reason, or cancelled), "skipped: ... (why)", "cancelled: ...". The
      studio's: the keys that start work ("key g (games screen): make the takes of the methods
      marked"), the choices and marks made, every message ("error: " before a refusal). Past 4
      MB the file moves to W/studio.log.1 (replacing the one there) and another starts.
      --sid-dir PATH   the SID collection to use (HVSC's C64Music, or any folder of .sid files)
                       instead of the one the studio downloads (below)
      --ay-dir PATH    the AY collection to use (a folder holding music/ay, or any folder of
                       .ay files) instead of the one the studio downloads (below)
      --offline        download nothing: SID takes need --sid-dir or a collection already in
                       build/hvsc/, AY takes --ay-dir or one in build/ay/; tools/arrange-music
                       runs with UV_OFFLINE=1 and generate --offline; tools/make-packages with
                       --offline (the SDK files from build/ac79/ only)
      --cache DIR      tools/make-packages --cache DIR: another folder for the SDK files
      --model NAME     the generator for AI takes (tools/arrange-music generate --model: amt, the
                       default, or rules)

  The methods, per game (marked in W/studio.toml):
      F  FM   the FM-1 plays the music learned from the game's code and memory: always there,
              take 0, "the original" (for a shadow score, the routine playing live)
      A  AI   arranged takes (a bass, a pad and a drum around the melody):
              tools/arrange-music generate W/all --game ID [--model NAME], takes take-<n>
      S  SID  the game's C64 tune: tools/arrange-music chip W/all --game ID --sid-dir PATH,
              takes chip-<n>
      Y  AY   the game's Spectrum 128 tune: tools/arrange-music ay W/all --game ID --ay-dir PATH,
              takes ay-<n>
  AI, SID and AY work on a game's scores (recorded or shadow scores, BUILDING.md "Arranged music"); a
  game with none has nothing to arrange: its music plays as FM from its code, or on the beeper.
  tools/arrange-music runs through uv (https://docs.astral.sh/uv/); its first AI job downloads
  its packages and a 1.4 GB model.

  The games screen: a row per game, its name, how it went in, its packed size (with its chosen
  music, as of the last learning), its pack (P1 to P9, a colour per pack, or a dash), its music
  methods ("FM AI SID AY": FM always, the others coloured when marked, dots when not), its
  scores at a glance (below), and per score the choice (original, take-2, chip-1, ay-1;
  "chip-1 (judge)" for one the judge made, "listen" where the judge is not sure and nothing is
  chosen), else "nothing to arrange: FM from code", "beeper", or "no music" (dim: no score, no
  shadow score, no routine learned; never to hear, nothing for the judge). Games left out are
  dimmed, with why. A game with work in hand shows its bar instead (above). The glance: "?2"
  (yellow) two scores to hear (the judge weighed their takes and is not
  sure, and nothing is chosen); "m" (dim) takes the judge has not chosen from yet (takes it
  has not weighed or that changed since, or a judge sure of its pick that has not chosen: m
  does); "✓" (green, "+"
  without UTF-8) every score with takes chosen, by you or by the judge; nothing for a game
  with no takes. The header says how many games have scores to hear ("3 games to hear (n: the
  next)"). Above the
  rows, the packs: per pack with games "P1  312.4/416.7 KB <bar> 4 games" (its games' bytes
  against what a pack holds: the room less 2% less the pack's overhead, the plan's bytes beyond
  its games, as the split counts), red with how much over when over; then the games in no pack
  ("sizes as of the last learn" when a take was chosen after it). The packs are kept in
  W/studio.toml [packs].
      Up/Down, j/k   move (PgUp/PgDn, Home/End)
      Enter          open the game
      1-9            put the game in that pack; one it would take over its room refuses it (red)
                     until the same key again puts it there anyway (then no build starts)
      0, x           the game in no pack
      f              every packed game in no pack into the first pack with room, in name order
      p              the packs: each pack, its fill and its games (1-9, 0/x move the game chosen,
                     Enter opens it, b builds, Left/h/Esc/p back)
      a / s / y      mark or unmark a way to get new music for the game (nothing runs yet):
                     a AI, the model writes a bass, chords and drums around its melody;
                     s SID, its C64 version's tune from the SID collection;
                     y AY, its Spectrum 128 version's tune from the AY collection
      A / S / Y      the same for every game with scores
      m              find and judge: marks SID and AY for the game, queues their jobs as g does
                     (the collections downloaded first when there are none; no AI model, no
                     GPU), then a judge job, tools/arrange-music judge W/all --game ID --apply,
                     after those: it weighs every take of the game's scores and chooses where
                     it is sure and you have not chosen; then the screen reads it all again. Its
                     bars on the game's row, the bottom line and in J, as for every job; for a
                     game with no score: nothing to find or judge (said)
      M              the same for every game with scores, game by game (each game's SID and AY
                     jobs and its judge before the next game's); with more than one game each
                     game's judge runs with --provisional and the judge's final pass,
                     tools/arrange-music judge W/all --apply (with --game for the games m was
                     given), follows them all: a score is measured against other games' takes,
                     and the pass leaves what one judge over them all leaves once every game's
                     takes are there (about a second when nothing changed; a game it settles
                     otherwise is said). A game with no score (no tune for another version to
                     replace) is skipped, and the log says so ("find and judge: skipped 13 games
                     with no music ...")
      n              the cursor to the next game with scores to hear (round to the top)
      g              generate the marked methods: an AI job per game with A, a SID job per game
                     with S, an AY job per game with Y (the SID or AY collection is downloaded
                     first when there is none); it says how many it queued ("queued 5 jobs (2 AI,
                     3 SID): J shows them"), and each game with a job has its bar (above)
      J              the jobs (below)
      r              learn again (the plan: sizes change with the choices)
      b              build the firmware: the packages (fmzx.fwsc, fmzx-preview.fwsc) of each pack,
                     with each game's chosen music, in W/pack-<n>/
      l              the log (W/studio.log); ? this text; q quit (asks when jobs are running)
  The studio opens on a start screen for a moment: a game's loading screen drawn in ZX pixels
  (the border's stripes while the picture loads by rows, then PRESS ANY KEY flashing); a key
  ends it and still acts on the games screen; FMZX_SPLASH=0 starts at the games screen. The
  border is a Spectrum's in proportion from 94x27 up, narrower below; under 72x20, no start screen.
  Colours (bold, dim and reverse only on a terminal without them, or with NO_COLOR set): names and
  titles bold, the chosen candidate green (blue when the judge chose it), marked methods AI
  magenta, SID cyan, AY yellow (dim when not), errors, refusals and a take's faults red, scores
  to hear yellow, the bar yellow while a step runs, green when the last one is done, red when
  one failed; while learning, ▸ yellow, ✓ green, ✓ from the cache dim green; on the jobs view,
  running yellow, done green, failed red, waiting dim; on the game screen a take's closeness to
  the original green from 50% (the same tune), yellow from 20% (part of it), dim under 20%
  (another tune), the judge's pick (#1) blue.

  The game screen: the game's scores (key, seconds, shadow or not, what the last plan says
  plays; on the line under it, for a score with takes, its state, "chosen by you", "chosen by
  the judge", "to hear", "the judge is sure: m chooses" or "not judged", and the judge's
  verdict, "judge: chip-1 (low: close call; winner not shown to be the same tune)", with
  "(takes changed since: m judges again)" when its judge.toml no longer fits the takes), and
  per score its candidates. Each take the judge
  weighed (W/all/arrange/<game>/<key>/judge.toml, written by tools/arrange-music) shows, before
  its own line, its place in the judge's order ("#1", the judge's pick, in blue), its
  closeness to the original ("close 97%": the same tune from 50%), its quality ("qual 62%";
  on a screen 140 columns wide or more its polyphony, harmony and rhythm too) and its faults
  in words, in red ("refused by the renderer: ...", "plays only a fragment: 1.5 s of the 14.5
  s that play", "a silent gap of 3.2 s"):
      beeper                  the game's own sound (to compare; not a choice)
      FM (from code/memory)   take 0, the original (no closeness: it is the tune)
      AI take-<n>             with its key, consonance and notes a second (arranged.toml [metrics])
      SID chip-<n>            its source
      C64 original (chip-<n>) the C64's own sound, when tools/arrange-music wrote chip.wav
      AY ay-<n>               its source
      Spectrum 128 original (ay-<n>)  the Spectrum 128's own sound, when tools/arrange-music
                              wrote ay.wav
  The choice is marked * (green) when you made it, j (blue) when the judge did.
  A game with no scores lists the whole game's beeper and FM, to listen to; for one with no
  music the line under its name says so, with what its renders tell: "No music found in this
  game: it plays sound effects from its own code." (a render has sound), "...: it makes no
  sound in its first 40 s." (the renders are silent), or "...: Enter renders its own sound, to
  hear any sound effects." (none rendered yet).
  Silence: a render (or a file tools/arrange-music wrote) is measured before it plays (Python's
  wave module, its peak against full scale); one whose peak is under 16/32768 is not played:
  the key line says "silent: this game makes no sound in its first 40 s" (a take: "silent:
  <label> makes no sound in its 14 s"), its row says "[silent: no sound in 40 s]" (dim), and
  Enter on it says so again. A preview's level is kept beside it (W/previews/<name>.wav.level:
  its peak and seconds), so it is not measured or rendered again until the preview is made
  again.
      Up/Down, j/k   move
      Enter, space   play the candidate, or stop it when it is the one playing (playing
                     another stops the one playing)
      Esc            stop (with nothing playing: back)
      c              choose the candidate for its score: written to W/all/arrange/choices.toml
                     at once (as tools/arrange-music pick --set does), as your choice (the
                     judge never changes it); the chosen one is marked * and the others not
      a / s / y / g  mark AI / SID / AY for this game, generate them (Y: AY for every game)
      m              find and judge, for this game (as on the games screen)
      Left, h, Backspace   back; l the log; J the jobs
  What plays: music-preview's render of exactly what the FM-1 plays (W/previews/<game>__<key>__
  <take>.wav, made in the background, "rendering..." until it is ready, then played; kept until
  the take changes); without music-preview, the renders tools/arrange-music wrote: original.wav
  (General MIDI, for take 0), take-<n>/fm1.wav, chip-<n>/fm1.wav, ay-<n>/fm1.wav. A candidate with nothing to
  play says why. The player is afplay on macOS, else ffplay, paplay or aplay; FMZX_PLAYER="cmd
  args" overrides (the file is added last).

  The jobs view (J on every screen; J, Esc, Left or h goes back): every job of the session, run
  one at a time in this order: the one running, those waiting (in the order they run; SID and AY
  takes held until their collection's download is done), then those finished, newest first. Per
  job its state (running m:ss yellow, waiting dim, done in N s green, failed red, cancelled, or
  skipped when a job before it in its chain failed or its collection did not download), what it
  is (AI, SID or AY takes, the method in its colour; a collection's download, the learning, a
  render, a choice, a pack's build), its game, and a line in words (never its output): while a
  job runs, a small bar of its own, then, for an AI job, "take 2 of 4,
  pass 'bass', 38% (about 24 min left)" (from tools/arrange-music generate's progress lines: the
  passes drums, bass, chords; the time left from the takes done, else its estimate), a SID or AY
  job "checking candidates: 3 of 12", a judge job "judging: 3 of 12 scores", a download its
  MB, the learning its header; when it is done, its result from tools/arrange-music's summary
  line (its docstring, "The summary line"): "3 takes, best closeness 73% (C64: <title> by
  <author> (<released>))" (with "weak: probably another tune, listen" under 20%), "no C64 tune
  found by that name", "0 takes, 4 songs skipped (48K beeper or CPC tunes)", "4 takes written,
  3 min 12 s", "had its SID takes already: nothing to do", for the judge "12 scores: 3 chosen
  by the judge (2 in this run), 4 by you, 5 to hear, 1 faulty take" (and "the renderer check skipped
  (no music-preview)" when it was); a failed job its reason in a line, its exit code and the
  last line of its output that reads as an error, without the tool's "tools/<name>: " (cut to
  70 characters), and where the rest is: "exit 1: no music-preview here (the rest in
  studio.log: l)". A finished takes or judge job also says its result on the key line ("SID
  takes for <game>: 3 (best closeness 73%); Enter on the game to listen", "judge, <game>: ...;
  n goes to the next game to hear"; a failed one its reason and the log's name). The jobs are
  the session's (their lines are in W/studio.log).
      Up/Down, j/k   move (PgUp/PgDn, Home/End)
      Enter          the job's lines from the log, scrollable (l: the whole log)
      o              open the job's game, to listen to its takes
      x              cancel: a waiting job leaves the queue at once; the running one, after y, is
                     stopped (SIGTERM to its process group, SIGKILL 5 s later; a download stops at
                     its next block) and marked cancelled

  The build (b, or --build): the plan again (choices change the packed sizes), then the packs
  filled with 1-9 (or f), each as W/pack-<n>/ under its own number (packs with no game skipped);
  a game in no pack is not built (the summary names them), and a pack over what it holds stops
  the build before it starts, naming the pack and by how much. With no game in any pack, the
  packed games in name order fill pack 1 while the total stays within the room minus 2% (and
  the pack's own overhead), then pack 2, and so on. Each pack is
  W/pack-<n>/: personal-games.toml (the header and those games' entries from the games file,
  W/all/personal-games.toml or the one given, as written there, [game.keys] and
  [game.instruments] tables included; a relative file made absolute), the links
  sound-cache -> ../all/sound-cache and arrange -> ../all/arrange (the learned music and the
  choices are shared), and the pack and its packages:
      fmzx-pack personal W/pack-<n>/personal-games.toml -o W/pack-<n> --app <fm1 app> --app <fm1-preview app>
      FMZX_SKIP_DEVICE_BUILD=1 FMZX_PACKAGES_PACK=W/pack-<n>/games.bin FMZX_PACKAGES_OUT=W/pack-<n> \
          tools/make-packages [--offline] [--cache DIR]
  which write the pack's games.bin, keymaps.json, fmzx.fwsc and fmzx-preview.fwsc (and the SDK
  files' licence text beside them), print the pack's table (each game, how it went in, its packed
  size; the room used and free; the music learned) and verify both packages. The build needs
  build/fm1/platform/fm1/fmzx_app.bin, build/fm1-preview/platform/fm1/fmzx_app.bin and
  build/fm1/third_party/felucca-loader/loader.bin (the device builds at start make them).
  A pack of the name-order split that fails with "the pack does not fit" gives its last game to
  the next pack and is built again; a game that does not fit in a pack of its own is left out of
  every pack, and the summary says so (a pack you filled that does not fit fails the build).
  The other packs the studio made (a W/pack-<m>/.pack-studio marker) are removed. The summary lists each pack, its games and its fmzx.fwsc; the studio prints
  it again when it closes. Install a pack's fmzx.fwsc as docs/install.md describes.

  The SID collection: with no --sid-dir, the studio uses build/hvsc/<version>/ (repo root),
  downloading it on the first SID job: the High Voltage SID Collection, version and address from
  https://hvsc.c64.org/api/v1/version/7z (about 85 MB, its MB on the bar), streamed to
  a .part file and renamed when complete, then unpacked into build/hvsc/<version>/ (about 380 MB,
  half a minute), the archive then deleted. bsdtar (libarchive: macOS's tar), else 7-Zip (7zz, 7z
  or 7za), lists the members first (tar -tf and tar -tvf, or 7z l -slt): a member whose path is
  absolute or holds "..", or that is a link or a device, refuses the whole archive, as does more
  than 8 GB unpacked; the same tool then unpacks it. Every path unpacked is checked to stay
  inside, any link is removed, and nothing in the archive is ever run. The collection is the
  folder holding DOCUMENTS/STIL.txt (C64Music). The tunes belong to their authors: keep the
  collection and the packs made with it to yourself (BUILDING.md, "Music from other versions").

  The AY collection: with no --ay-dir, the studio uses build/ay/<commit>/music/ay (repo root),
  downloading it on the first AY job: the chris-y/AY repository on GitHub (Spectrum AY music;
  music/ay/games/ holds the games' tunes, music/ay/misc/ more), its newest commit from
  https://api.github.com/repos/chris-y/AY/commits/main, its archive from
  https://codeload.github.com/chris-y/AY/tar.gz/<commit> (about 30 MB, its MB on the bar),
  streamed to a .part file and renamed when complete, then read as a stream: only the
  members under music/ay are unpacked, into build/ay/<first 12 digits of the commit>/; a member
  whose path is absolute or holds "..", or one under music/ay that is a link or a device,
  refuses the whole archive; every path unpacked is checked to stay inside, the archive is then
  deleted, and nothing in it is ever run. The same holds for these tunes: keep them and the
  packs made with them to yourself.

  Without the screen: any of --assign, --auto-fill, --generate, --auto, --build, --status and
  --try (several run in that order; a step that fails stops the command, exit 1, with its
  reason in a line and the log's path: "tools/pack-studio: --generate: AI takes for <game>
  failed: exit 1: ... (the log: W/studio.log)"). They take the options above. Every tool's line
  goes to the log. On a terminal (stdout), while steps run, a line per game as its work goes: a
